<section class="article-header wrap-article">
    <p class="section-label">Opinion</p>
    <h1>The Employment Tribunal Runs on Who Can Afford to Find Out Who's Right</h1>
    <div class="byline-row">
        <img src="/assets/images/grace-pariser-avatar.webp" alt="Grace Pariser" class="byline-avatar">
        <p class="byline">By Grace Pariser &middot; September 2026</p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section wrap-article" style="border-top:none;">
    <div class="prose dropcap">
        <p>The Ministry of Justice published its latest tribunal statistics in June, and the numbers tell you most of what you need to know before you get to a single case. There were 531,000 open employment tribunal claims at the end of March 2026, up from 491,000 a year earlier. Single claims reached 64,000, a rise of 55% year on year. The tribunal received 22,000 new claims in the quarter and closed just 11,000. No one in government is pretending this backlog is coming down soon.</p>
        <p>I spend most weeks on the other side of that statistic, and the call rarely comes after an ET1 has been filed. It comes at the time of the incident, when an employer wants to know whether they're better off settling now or letting it run. The honest answer is that the merits of the case are often the smaller part of that decision. What actually drives it has nothing to do with who did what to whom. It comes down to who ends up exposed to the financial risk of finding out.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="pull-quote">
        <p>What actually drives it has nothing to do with who did what to whom. It comes down to who ends up exposed to the financial risk of finding out.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
        <p>Start with what it costs an employer to defend a claim through to a final hearing. Newtons, a firm that publishes its pricing, puts a simple case at &pound;4,500 to &pound;8,000 plus VAT, a medium complexity case at &pound;8,000 to &pound;17,000, and a high complexity case at &pound;17,000 to &pound;36,000. Other firms quote similar bands, and discrimination or whistleblowing claims regularly push past &pound;50,000 once experts and barristers are involved. None of that is recoverable if you win. Employment tribunals operate on the basis that each side pays its own costs, full stop, so a successful defence still leaves the employer several thousand pounds worse off than they were before the claim arrived.</p>
        <p>Now look at what the claimant risks. Costs orders against a losing claimant are, in practice, close to theoretical. In 2019/2020, 49,998 claims were disposed of in the employment tribunals and only 177 costs orders were made. Other estimates put the figure at around 1% of losing parties. The threshold for one, "vexatious, abusive, disruptive or otherwise unreasonable" conduct, is set deliberately high, because the tribunal was built as an accessible forum for people who couldn't afford lawyers. That's a good and defensible principle. It's also the reason a claimant with a thin case and nothing to lose can bring one anyway, and the employer on the other end faces a bill regardless of the outcome.</p>
        <p>Into that gap has walked generative AI, and faster than most of the profession expected. Employment lawyers are now reporting, consistently, that claimants are using ChatGPT and similar tools to draft grievances and tribunal pleadings that would previously have needed a solicitor or a union rep to produce. One firm described the result as arming litigants in person with a highly productive, if sometimes badly misguided, assistant. A barrister writing on this recently noted that while the tools give people an accessible route into proceedings, the claims that come out the other end often look comprehensive while lacking the legal structure or factual precision to actually succeed. That's the double edge of it. The access-to-justice case for AI is real, and I don't want to wave it away just because it's inconvenient for my clients (I've read enough AI-drafted grievances now to know exactly which paragraph came from the prompt and which came from the employee, and it's rarely a flattering comparison for the AI).</p>
        <p>But the practical effect for an employer is that bringing a claim, in time and confidence rather than money, has got cheaper at the exact moment the volume of claims is climbing fastest. Whistleblowing claims are up 112% year on year. Disability discrimination claims are up 79%. Unfair dismissal remains the single largest category, and the Employment Rights Act 2025 is going to widen who can bring one of those, not narrow it. None of this is a coincidence, and none of it is going away before it gets worse.</p>
        <p>None of this means most claims are speculative. Most aren't. But a share of them are filed by people who know, or ought to know, that they don't have a case, and who correctly work out that trying costs them nothing while the employer pays regardless. That risk doesn't stop with the employer. It falls on every claimant with a genuine grievance sitting behind it in the queue. The tribunal's open caseload had reached 831,000 by December 2025, the highest it's been since 2013/14, and some hearings are now being listed as far out as 2029. A speculative claim that goes through early conciliation and gets filed anyway takes up a slot in that queue exactly like a genuine one does. The backlog can't tell the difference.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="pull-quote">
        <p>A speculative claim that goes through early conciliation and gets filed anyway takes up a slot in that queue exactly like a genuine one does. The backlog can't tell the difference.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
        <p>The employee who was genuinely mistreated is the one actually harmed, left waiting years for anyone to hear the case.</p>
        <p>Acas is buckling under the same pressure, and the government's fix has been to give everyone longer, not to move things faster. Acas handled more than 117,000 early conciliation cases in 2024/25, the highest number since the pandemic and 13,000 more than the year before. From 1 December 2025, the maximum conciliation period doubled from six weeks to twelve, and the explanatory memorandum for the change says this plainly: it's there to ease the pressure on Acas caused by rising demand and increasingly complex cases, not to improve anyone's chances of settling. The government also plans to extend the time limit for bringing a claim from three months to six from October 2026. Combined with the conciliation change, a claimant could sit on a dispute for the better part of a year before the employer even knows a claim is coming. That's more breathing room for everyone with a genuine grievance to get advice and think it through, which is a fair aim on its own terms. It's also longer for a backlog that was already record-breaking to keep growing, which does nothing for the claimant with a real case who's already waiting behind it.</p>
        <p>What does an employer actually get if the claim fails? An average unfair dismissal award in 2023/24 was &pound;13,749, with a median of just &pound;6,746. Modest, in other words, against the legal spend needed to get there. Discrimination awards are a different animal entirely, uncapped, and the highest sex discrimination award that year reached &pound;995,128. So the range an employer is defending against runs from "barely worth the legal fees either way" to "existential", and they often can't tell which one they're in until well into the process.</p>
        <p>This is where I tell clients, consistently, to treat tribunal exposure as a business risk, not just a legal one. Employment practices legal expenses insurance, usually bundled with an advice line, exists precisely to convert an unpredictable five-figure legal bill into a fixed annual premium. It doesn't touch the compensation risk, and it won't stop a bad claim being filed. What it does is take the "can we afford to defend this properly" question off the table before the ET1 has even been filed, which is the moment most employers are least equipped to think clearly about cost.</p>
        <p>None of this is an argument against the tribunal system existing, and I'd be uncomfortable if it read that way. Genuinely mistreated employees need somewhere to go that doesn't require a war chest to access, and a system with no costs risk for claimants is, structurally, the system that achieves that. The principle is sound. The same design that protects a genuine claimant with no money also protects a speculative one, and there's currently nothing in the process that tells the two apart before the employer has already spent most of the &pound;15,000 to &pound;40,000 it takes to find out which kind they're dealing with.</p>
        <p>Employers who understand that distinction ask a more useful question: what does it cost us either way, and have we priced that risk before it arrives? That's the question worth answering before the next ET1 reaches your desk, not after.</p>
    </div>
</section>

